// Module 02, Exercise 02: Reading and Writing Memory
//
// In C, you dereference a pointer to read/write:
//   *p = 42;        // write
//   int val = *p;   // read
//
// In crunch-c:
//   p.poke(i32, 42)   // write an i32
//   val = p.peek(i32) // read an i32
//
// You can also access individual bytes with p[i].

// Allocate 4 bytes and write an i32 to it.
p = garrison(4)
p.poke(i32, 42)
print(f"wrote 42")

// Read it back.
val = p.peek(i32)
print(f"read: {val}")

// Write a different value.
p.poke(i32, 999)
val = p.peek(i32)
print(f"read again: {val}")

// Byte access — read individual bytes.
byte_val = p[0]
print(f"first byte: {byte_val}")

// YOUR TASK:
// 1. What is the first byte of the i32 value 42?
// 2. Write i32(1000) and read back the first two bytes.
//    What values do you get? (Hint: think about endianness)
// 3. Allocate 8 bytes, write two i32 values, and read them back.
